{#
   Rust module index (`mod.rs`) for a generated state machine directory.

   It exists because Rust resolves a sibling module through its parent
   `mod.rs`, unlike C++ (`-I`) or Kotlin (package path). The file is a
   generated artifact and carries the drift header, so the traceability
   contract requires it to carry an SCE-MAP marker as well.

   The marker names the machine symbol, at the machine's own source
   location. It renders through `_macros/sce_map_marker.jinja2` like
   every other marker in the tree: a hand-written marker string in the
   emitter is a second copy of the shape AND a second copy of the
   location, and the copy that lived here drifted — it cited line 1
   unconditionally, which is the XML declaration, not the `<scxml>`
   element the `_machine` symbol resolves to.
#}
{#
   `module_path`, when set, is a Rust string literal naming the module
   file. It is what an `OUT_DIR` artifact needs and a source-tree sibling
   does not: `mod x_sm;` finds a sibling by position, and an artifact
   Cargo put in `OUT_DIR` has no position relative to the consumer's
   source. A consumer cannot write the `#[path]` themselves either —
   built-in attributes take a string literal, so `#[path = concat!(env!(
   "OUT_DIR"), …)]` does not expand — which is why `compile_scxml` writes
   this file: `build.rs` is the one place that knows `OUT_DIR` as a value
   and can spell it as a literal.

   Both forms render here, and the two lines below are why: `mod x_sm;
   pub use x_sm::*;` is the shape a consumer's names resolve through, and
   a second emitter would be a second copy of it.

   The `#[path]` form carries no SCE-MAP marker. The module-level marker
   is an INNER attribute (`#![doc = …]`), which is exactly what an
   `include!` of this file would reject — and it would be a second
   declaration of a location the module file already declares, which the
   consumer now receives intact.
#}
{#
   The head is built as a value rather than as two template blocks. A
   block-level `{% if %}` here has to place its own newlines around a
   line that is a `//` comment, and getting that wrong does not fail to
   render — it emits `// GENERATED …#[path = …]mod x_sm;`, where the
   comment swallows both items and only the `pub use` survives to be
   reported, one line further down, as an unresolved module.
#}
{%- import '_macros/sce_map_marker.jinja2' as sce_map -%}
{%- if module_path -%}
{%- set head = "#[path = " ~ module_path ~ "]\n" -%}
{%- else -%}
{%- set head = sce_map.source_marker(model.source_location, "rust", module_level=true, symbol_artifact=sce_artifact.machine) ~ "\n\n" -%}
{%- endif -%}
// GENERATED -- DO NOT EDIT (sce-codegen)
{{ head }}mod {{ module_stem }}_sm;
pub use {{ module_stem }}_sm::*;
